**Theorem 12.2 (at most five complete pure contacts).** Under
Assumption 10.1, the entire pure-sheet inventory has at most five
distinct directed points.

Proof. By Theorem 11.1, a pure contact of nonpositive height must
have $u<0$. Theorem 12.1 therefore puts the complete pure inventory
in the union of just three tests:
$F_{0,+,+}$, $F_{0,-,+}$ and $F_{0,-,-}$. Zero-height contacts
may occur in two descriptions, which only lowers the union count.
Each test has at most two distinct zeros. Six distinct contacts
would force all three tests to have two distinct zeros.

Apply Theorem 12.1 to the first two tests. Write their squares as
$(a_\epsilon\cdot x+C_\epsilon)^2$ with
$a_\epsilon\in\mathbb R^2$. Two zeros confined to one open
$u$-hemisphere imply $C_\epsilon\ne0$, $a_\epsilon\ne0$,
and $|C_\epsilon|<\|a_\epsilon\|$: a zero constant gives
antipodal zeros, and equality gives at most one circle zero.
Their common even part has the uniquely represented symmetric matrix

$$
H=a_\epsilon a_\epsilon^T+C_\epsilon^2I_2.
\tag{FC.38}
$$

Equality of the quadratic functions on the unit circle implies
matrix equality, so $H$ is the same for both signs. Its eigenvalues
are $C_\epsilon^2$ and $C_\epsilon^2+\|a_\epsilon\|^2$.
The top eigenline is unique; thus the magnitudes and eigenline of
the two nonzero odd vectors $2C_\epsilon a_\epsilon$ agree.
Those vectors are either equal or opposite. Their difference is
$-2\lambda(1,0)^T$, so they must be opposite and parallel to the
$u$ axis. With $A,B>0$ the squares are consequently

$$
F_{0,\epsilon,+}=(Au-\epsilon B)^2,\qquad
2AB=\lambda,\qquad P_y-\ell=A^2u^2+B^2.
\tag{FC.39}
$$

The remaining test is
$F_{0,-,-}=A^2u^2+B^2+2\ell+\lambda u$.
Its even matrix is
$H_-=\operatorname{diag}(A^2,0)+B^2I_2+2E_\ell$, whose
off-diagonal entry is $2E_{\ell,12}<0$. Its odd vector is
$(2l_u+\lambda,2l_v)^T$, with both coordinates strictly positive.
If this test also had two zeros, its square representation and
(FC.38) would make that odd vector parallel to its top eigenvector.
For a real symmetric matrix with strictly negative off-diagonal,
its largest eigenvalue is strictly greater than either diagonal
entry, and its eigenvector coordinates have opposite signs:
$(\lambda_{\max}-(H_-)_{11})z_1=(H_-)_{12}z_2$. A vector with two
strictly positive coordinates cannot be parallel to it. This
contradiction excludes six contacts. $\square$

